<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>8.4.3: Applying the Zero Product Property to Solve a Real-World Projectile Problem</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02145</md:content-id>
    <md:title>8.4.3: Applying the Zero Product Property to Solve a Real-World Projectile Problem</md:title>
    <md:uuid>cee0a498-42ab-4363-ac04-6a5f11a4d4d3</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity</title>
<para> We have seen quadratic functions modeling the height of a projectile as a function of time.</para>
<para>Here are two ways to define the same function that approximates the height of a projectile in meters, \(t\) seconds after launch:</para>
<para>\(h(t)=-5t^2+27t+18\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;h(t)=(-5t−3)(t−6)\)</para>
<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Which way of defining the function allows us to use the <term data-schema-version="1.0" data-store="glossary-tooltip">zero product property</term> to find out when the height of the object is 0 meters (when the object is on the ground)?</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your explanation.</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para> The one with a quadratic expression in factored form.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->
<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Without graphing, determine at what time(s) the height of the object is 0 meters (when the object is on the ground). Show your reasoning.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter the times(s) and your reasoning.</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para> The object has a height of 0 meters after 6 seconds. Applying the zero product property to solve \((-5t−3)(t−6 )=0\) gives \(t=- \frac {3}{5}\) and \(t=6\). Because the object was launched at \(t=0\), the negative solution doesn’t make sense in this context.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->
<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Using the function in standard form, determine the height of the object before it started its motion, at \(t=0\) seconds. Be prepared to show your reasoning.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter the height of the object and your reasoning.</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para> The object has a height of 18 meters at 0 seconds. By substituting 0 for \(t\) into the quadratic form, the starting height can be determined. \(h(0)=-5(0)^2+27(0)+18=18\).</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->
